              semantics to the _m_o_d_e argument of ooppeennaatt(2).

              Whereas  ooppeennaatt(2)  ignores  bits  other than those in the range
              _0_7_7_7_7 in its  _m_o_d_e  argument,  ooppeennaatt22()  returns  an  error  if
              _h_o_w_._m_o_d_e contains bits other than _0_7_7_7_7.  Similarly, an error is
              returned  if  ooppeennaatt22()  is  called  with a nonzero _h_o_w_._m_o_d_e and
              _h_o_w_._f_l_a_g_s does not contain OO__CCRREEAATT or OO__TTMMPPFFIILLEE.

       _r_e_s_o_l_v_e
              This is a bit‐mask of flags that modify the  way  in  which  aallll
              components  of  _p_a_t_h  will be resolved.  (See ppaatthh__rreessoolluuttiioonn(7)
              for background information.)

              The primary use case for these flags is to  allow  trusted  pro‐
              grams to restrict how untrusted paths (or paths inside untrusted
              directories) are resolved.  The full list of _r_e_s_o_l_v_e flags is as
              follows:

              RREESSOOLLVVEE__BBEENNEEAATTHH
                     Do  not permit the path resolution to succeed if any com‐
                     ponent of the resolution is not a descendant of  the  di‐
                     rectory  indicated  by  _d_i_r_f_d.  This causes absolute sym‐
                     bolic links (and absolute values of _p_a_t_h) to be rejected.

                     Currently, this flag also disables magic‐link  resolution
                     (see  below).   However,  this  may change in the future.
                     Therefore, to ensure that magic links are  not  resolved,
                     the  caller  should  explicitly  specify RREESSOOLLVVEE__NNOO__MMAAGGII‐‐
                     CCLLIINNKKSS.

              RREESSOOLLVVEE__IINN__RROOOOTT
                     Treat the directory referred to by _d_i_r_f_d as the root  di‐
                     rectory  while  resolving  _p_a_t_h.  Absolute symbolic links
                     are interpreted relative to _d_i_r_f_d.  If a prefix component
                     of _p_a_t_h equates to _d_i_r_f_d, then an  immediately  following
                     _._.  component  likewise  equates to _d_i_r_f_d (just as _/_._. is
                     traditionally equivalent to _/).  If _p_a_t_h is absolute,  it
                     is also interpreted relative to _d_i_r_f_d.

                     The  effect of this flag is as though the calling process
                     had used cchhrroooott(2) to (temporarily) modify its  root  di‐
                     rectory  (to  the  directory referred to by _d_i_r_f_d).  How‐
                     ever, unlike cchhrroooott(2) (which changes the filesystem root
                     permanently for a process), RREESSOOLLVVEE__IINN__RROOOOTT allows a pro‐
                     gram to efficiently restrict path resolution  on  a  per‐
                     open basis.

                     Currently, this flag also disables magic‐link resolution.
                     However,  this  may  change in the future.  Therefore, to
                     ensure that magic links  are  not  resolved,  the  caller
                     should explicitly specify RREESSOOLLVVEE__NNOO__MMAAGGIICCLLIINNKKSS.

              RREESSOOLLVVEE__NNOO__MMAAGGIICCLLIINNKKSS
                     Disallow  all  magic‐link  resolution during path resolu‐
                     tion.

                     Magic links are symbolic link‐like objects that are  most
                     notably  found in pprroocc(5); examples include _/_p_r_o_c_/pid_/_e_x_e
                     and _/_p_r_o_c_/pid_/_f_d_/_*.  (See ssyymmlliinnkk(7) for more details.)

                     Unknowingly opening magic links can be risky for some ap‐
                     plications.  Examples of such risks include  the  follow‐
                     ing:

                     •  If  the  process  opening  a pathname is a controlling
                        process that currently  has  no  controlling  terminal
                        (see ccrreeddeennttiiaallss(7)), then opening a magic link inside
                        _/_p_r_o_c_/pid_/_f_d that happens to refer to a terminal would
                        cause the process to acquire a controlling terminal.

                     •  In  a  containerized  environment, a magic link inside
                        _/_p_r_o_c may refer to an object  outside  the  container,
                        and  thus  may provide a means to escape from the con‐
                        tainer.

                     Because of such risks, an application may prefer to  dis‐
                     able  magic  link  resolution  using the RREESSOOLLVVEE__NNOO__MMAAGGII‐‐
                     CCLLIINNKKSS flag.

                     If the trailing component (i.e., basename) of _p_a_t_h  is  a
                     magic  link,  _h_o_w_._r_e_s_o_l_v_e contains RREESSOOLLVVEE__NNOO__MMAAGGIICCLLIINNKKSS,
                     and _h_o_w_._f_l_a_g_s contains both OO__PPAATTHH and  OO__NNOOFFOOLLLLOOWW,  then
                     an OO__PPAATTHH file descriptor referencing the magic link will
                     be returned.

              RREESSOOLLVVEE__NNOO__SSYYMMLLIINNKKSS
                     Disallow resolution of symbolic links during path resolu‐
                     tion.  This option implies RREESSOOLLVVEE__NNOO__MMAAGGIICCLLIINNKKSS.

                     If  the  trailing component (i.e., basename) of _p_a_t_h is a
                     symbolic link, _h_o_w_._r_e_s_o_l_v_e contains  RREESSOOLLVVEE__NNOO__SSYYMMLLIINNKKSS,
                     and  _h_o_w_._f_l_a_g_s  contains both OO__PPAATTHH and OO__NNOOFFOOLLLLOOWW, then
                     an OO__PPAATTHH file descriptor referencing the  symbolic  link
                     will be returned.

                     Note  that  the  effect  of the RREESSOOLLVVEE__NNOO__SSYYMMLLIINNKKSS flag,
                     which affects the treatment of symbolic links in  all  of
                     the  components  of  _p_a_t_h, differs from the effect of the
                     OO__NNOOFFOOLLLLOOWW file creation flag (in _h_o_w_._f_l_a_g_s),  which  af‐
                     fects  the  handling  of symbolic links only in the final
                     component of _p_a_t_h.

                     Applications that employ the RREESSOOLLVVEE__NNOO__SSYYMMLLIINNKKSS flag are
                     encouraged to make its use  configurable  (unless  it  is
                     used  for a specific security purpose), as symbolic links
                     are very widely used by end‐users.  Setting this flag in‐
                     discriminately—i.e., for purposes  not  specifically  re‐
                     lated to security—for all uses of ooppeennaatt22() may result in
                     spurious  errors  on previously functional systems.  This
                     may occur if, for example, a system pathname that is used
                     by an application is modified (e.g., in a  new  distribu‐
                     tion release) so that a pathname component (now) contains
                     a symbolic link.

              RREESSOOLLVVEE__NNOO__XXDDEEVV
                     Disallow traversal of mount points during path resolution
                     (including all bind mounts).  Consequently, _p_a_t_h must ei‐
                     ther be on the same mount as the directory referred to by
                     _d_i_r_f_d, or on the same mount as the current working direc‐
                     tory if _d_i_r_f_d is specified as AATT__FFDDCCWWDD.

                     Applications that employ the RREESSOOLLVVEE__NNOO__XXDDEEVV flag are en‐
                     couraged  to make its use configurable (unless it is used
                     for a specific security  purpose),  as  bind  mounts  are
                     widely  used by end‐users.  Setting this flag indiscrimi‐
                     nately—i.e., for purposes not specifically related to se‐
                     curity—for all uses of ooppeennaatt22() may result  in  spurious
                     errors  on previously functional systems.  This may occur
                     if, for example, a system pathname that is used by an ap‐
                     plication is modified (e.g., in a  new  distribution  re‐
                     lease) so that a pathname component (now) contains a bind
                     mount.

              RREESSOOLLVVEE__CCAACCHHEEDD (since Linux 5.12)
                     Make  the  open operation fail unless all path components
                     are already present in the kernel’s lookup cache.  If any
                     kind of revalidation or I/O  is  needed  to  satisfy  the
